.TH P256TEST 1
.SH NAME
p256test \- check p256(2) against another library, and time it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B p256test
[
.I reps
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I P256test
checks
.IR p256 (2)
\(em the NIST curve arithmetic this TLS client verifies certificate
chains with \(em against vectors produced by OpenSSL 3.6.3, and reports
how long a verification takes.
.PP
The vectors are two key pairs per curve, the ECDH shared secret each
way, and an ECDSA signature over a fixed message with the digest
matched to the curve: SHA-256 for P-256 and SHA-384 for P-384.  Both
curves are checked because a certificate chain in the wild has a P-256
leaf under a P-384 intermediate, so a client that could only do one of
them would fail on real traffic.
.PP
Every positive check has a negative control beside it: the same
verification with one byte changed in the hash, in
.IR r ,
in
.IR s ,
or under the wrong public key, and a point moved off the curve.  A
verifier that answers yes to everything passes the positive checks
alone, so the controls are what makes the positives mean anything.
.PP
.I Reps
sets how many verifications and agreements the timing does; one by
default.  The timing is the reason to keep this command: certificate
verification is where a handshake spends its time, and this says what
one costs on the machine in hand.  Run it interpreted \(em
.B emu
.B -c0
\(em for a figure that means anything about a native kernel, which has
no JIT.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/p256test.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR chainbench (1),
.IR p256 (2),
.IR tls12 (2)
.SH BUGS
The vectors are compiled in.  Refreshing them means running OpenSSL
again and editing the constants at the top of the source.
